<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/intermediate/the-league-of-nations">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This intermediate History lesson builds on the foundational <em>The World Wars</em> —
    the war that made the League and the war that ended it — and its sibling <em>The
    Treaty of Versailles</em>, whose settlement the Covenant was written into. It is
    organised around the exam's focus points: watch for judgements, not just narrative.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>describe the League's machinery — Council, Assembly, Secretariat, Court — and the absences that weakened it;</li>
    <li>judge the 1920s peacekeeping record case by case, including the humanitarian work;</li>
    <li>explain how the Depression changed the League's operating conditions;</li>
    <li>analyse the Manchuria and Abyssinia failures as evidence about will rather than design;</li>
    <li>argue a "to what extent" verdict with evidence on both sides.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The League of Nations was the most ambitious experiment in world government anyone had
    ever tried: fifty-plus states agreeing, in writing, to settle their disputes in a
    committee room in Geneva instead of on a battlefield. It failed at its one great job —
    there was a bigger war twenty years after the treaty that created it — and the exam
    question this page answers is <strong>to what extent the League was a success</strong>,
    argued through the syllabus's four focus points: judge its organisation and membership,
    its 1920s peacekeeping record, the damage the Depression did, and the 1930s failures in
    Manchuria and Abyssinia. The thesis to test as you read: <strong>the League was a working
    machine that the great powers stopped operating</strong>. Where the machine was actually
    run — by and for small states — it worked; where running it would cost a great power
    something real, it broke. Your essay grade depends on saying precisely where each half
    of that sentence is true.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="bet before you read on — where the machine ran" widget="predict" config={lonThesisPredict} />

  <Widget no="1" caption="how the League was built — and where it was weak" widget="equationStep" config={machineCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the cards move from blueprint to verdict: card 1 the
    machine as designed; card 2 who never sat in it; card 3 the unanimity flaw; card 4 the
    humanitarian record not to forget; card 5 the judgement the focus points ask for. Note
    the pattern: machinery that succeeded in 1921 and 1925 failed in 1931 and 1935 — the
    design did not change, the will did.
  </p>

  <h2>Did weaknesses in organisation and membership make failure inevitable?</h2>
  <p>
    The Covenant, which came into force in January 1920 as part of every peace treaty, built
    the machine: a <strong>Council</strong> of the great powers (permanent seats for Britain,
    France, Italy and Japan, plus elected members, with Germany added from 1926 and the USSR
    from 1934); an <strong>Assembly</strong> in which every member had one vote; a
    <strong>Secretariat</strong> to run the paperwork of a world foreign ministry; and from
    1922 the Permanent Court of International Justice at The Hague. Article 16 held the
    promise together: any member going to war in breach of the Covenant would face the
    collective trade embargo of every other member and, as a last resort, their combined
    armies. Now check the absences. The United States never joined — the Senate rejected the
    treaty in November 1919 and again in March 1920, so the institution's own architect stood
    outside it. Soviet Russia was excluded as a pariah until 1934. Germany was kept out until
    1926. And the two members who mattered most, Britain and France, were exhausted,
    financially strained, and — critically — disagreed with each other about what the League
    was for: Britain saw it as a talking shop that should not constrain imperial freedom;
    France saw it as a security alliance against Germany that should have had an army. The
    procedural design made things worse: every decision needed unanimity, and the League
    owned no soldiers — "force" meant members volunteering to fight someone else's war.
    Why build it that way? Because the drafters were frightened of being outvoted: unanimity
    protected every small state from being conscripted into great-power wars, and no member
    was willing to promise troops in advance for disputes it had not voted on. The design
    was not stupidity — it was the maximum sovereignty the members would surrender in 1919,
    which was less than collective security actually required.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair each organ with its job" widget="match" config={lonOrganMatch} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the League was doomed from the start, a bad design that
    could never work. The record contradicts this. The same League machinery resolved real
    disputes successfully in 1921 and 1925; the successor institution built in 1945 — the
    United Nations — kept nearly the whole design and works, imperfectly, to this day,
    fixing mainly the unanimity flaw with a Security Council veto. Design set the ceiling;
    political will decided whether it was reached. Weaknesses made failure <em>likely</em> —
    they meant the League could never outmuscle a determined great power — but "inevitable"
    is too strong, and essays should say so.
  </p>

    is too strong, and essays should say so.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the League failed because it had no army. The deeper gap
    was authority no one had surrendered: unanimity for every decision, and no member
    would promise troops for disputes it had not voted on. The design reflected the maximum
    sovereignty states would surrender in 1919 — the machine worked when members ran it
    and was helpless when they did not.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="draft the Covenant yourself, 1919" widget="branch" config={lonDraftingBranch} />

  <Widget no="2" caption="peacekeeping and its collapse, 1920–46 — chips separate the decades" widget="timeline" config={timelineCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the chips separate the decades — that split is the
    argument. The 1920s alternate failures (Vilna, Corfu) with real successes (Aaland,
    Upper Silesia, Greece-Bulgaria); after 1931 every entry is a collapse. At each entry
    ask: a small state that wanted resolving, or a great power determined not to be?
  </p>

  <h2>How successful was peacekeeping in the 1920s?</h2>
  <p>
    The 1920s record needs judging case by case, because the pattern it reveals is the
    answer. <strong>Vilna (1920)</strong> failed: Poland simply seized the Lithuanian capital,
    the League protested, Poland ignored it, and the great powers handed the matter to the
    Conference of Ambassadors — proof that a determined state with great-power friends could
    get away with it. <strong>Corfu (1923)</strong> failed the same way at higher stakes:
    when Italian general Tellini was murdered on Greek soil, Mussolini bombarded and occupied
    Corfu; Greece appealed to the League, but the dispute was settled around it, and Greece
    paid Italy an indemnity of 50 million lire. Against these, the successes were genuine.
    The <strong>Aaland Islands (1921)</strong> were awarded to Finland with a demilitarisation
    order — and both claimants accepted. <strong>Upper Silesia (1921)</strong>: a plebiscite
    split roughly 60–40 for Germany, and the League engineered a partition so sensible that
    Germany and Poland both accepted it in 1922. <strong>Greece–Bulgaria (1925)</strong> was
    the system working exactly as designed: after a border shooting, Greece invaded Bulgaria;
    the Council ordered withdrawal and compensation; Greece, a small state, complied and paid
    £45,000. Read the pattern honestly: the League could resolve almost any dispute between
    states that <em>wanted</em> resolving, and none involving a great power willing to use
    force. That is a real achievement and a fatal limitation at once. A 1924 attempt to close
    the gap — the Geneva Protocol, which would have made arbitration compulsory for all
    disputes — was killed by Britain as soon as a British government decided it might one
    day be applied to the Empire: the strongest members wanted a League that constrained
    others, and the smaller states knew it.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="line up the cases, earliest first" widget="order" config={lonCasesOrder} />
  <p>
    The humanitarian and technical record is easier to defend still, and examiners reward it
    precisely because it is often forgotten. The League repatriated hundreds of thousands of
    prisoners of war after 1918; Fridtjof Nansen's "Nansen passport" gave stateless refugees
    identity papers (Nobel Peace Prize, 1922); the Health Organisation ran campaigns against
    typhus, malaria and leprosy that became the seed of today's World Health Organization;
    the International Labour Organisation pushed the 48-hour week; and a 1926 convention
    attacked slavery. This work saved lives in every decade the League existed, and it is
    the strongest evidence that "failure" is too blunt a verdict. Note also what the
    peacemaking work had to build with: the League administered the Saar and Danzig directly,
    ran the referendums that sorted contested borders, and supervised the mandates —
    bureaucratic, unglamorous statecraft that mostly worked.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="file each quiet achievement" widget="sort-buckets" config={lonHumanBuckets} />

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the 1920s show a machine that worked for small states, not
    determined great powers — while humanitarian work saved lives all decade. Next: what
    changed the conditions for everyone.
  </p>

  <h2>How far did the Depression make the League's work harder?</h2>
  <p>
    The Wall Street Crash of October 1929 and the collapse of world trade that followed
    (global trade fell by roughly two-thirds in value between 1929 and 1932) changed the
    League's world. Governments everywhere raised tariffs and pursued economic nationalism;
    cooperation became expensive exactly when problems became more dangerous; and the
    politics of every major member turned inward. The banking crises of 1931 (Austria's
    Credit-Anstalt, then Germany and Britain leaving the gold standard) put every treasury
    on a war footing against its own economy. The Depression made aggressors too:
    Japan's militarists argued that a resource-poor Japan must grab an empire or starve, and
    Italy's Mussolini sought cheap imperial glory to distract from economic pain. It also
    disarmed the peacemakers: Britain and France, cutting budgets, had no money for
    sanctions or fleets, and their publics — remembering the Somme — would not support
    rearmament, let alone war. The Geneva Disarmament Conference of 1932–34, the League's
    last great hope, collapsed in this atmosphere when Germany walked out. Finally, the
    Depression destroyed the League's economic foundation: the Dawes-loan system that had
    stabilised Europe rested on American credit, and that credit died in 1929. The question
    "how far?" deserves a precise answer: the Depression did not break the League's rules or
    machinery — it removed the will, the money and the atmosphere of cooperation those rules
    depended on.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="call the collapse — world trade's value" widget="estimate" config={lonCrashEstimate} />

  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the Depression was an American economic event that
    stayed on Wall Street. It reached Geneva faster than any army: world trade fell by
    roughly two-thirds between 1929 and 1932, the 1931 banking crises put treasuries on a
    war footing, and the Dawes-loan system died with American credit. Cooperation became
    expensive exactly when problems grew more dangerous — which is why the failures begin
    in economics, not on a battlefield.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the Depression removed the will, the money and the cooperative
    atmosphere — not the rules or the machinery. Next, two great failures test what was
    left when an aggressor called the bluff.
  </p>

  <h2>Why did the League fail in the 1930s?</h2>
  <p>
    The two great failures teach the same lesson from two directions. In
    <strong>Manchuria (1931–33)</strong>, Japanese army officers staged an "incident" on the
    South Manchuria railway and their army conquered a province larger than France and
    Germany combined, renaming it Manchukuo under the puppet emperor Puyi. The League's
    response was procedural: a commission of inquiry (the Lytton Report, over a year in the
    making), an Assembly condemnation (February 1933) — and Japan simply resigned from the
    League in March. No sanctions were
    even proposed: Britain's navy was in home waters, the Depression made trade war
    unthinkable, and no one wanted to fight Japan in Manchuria. The lesson the world drew:
    the League would condemn and do nothing. In <strong>Abyssinia (1935–36)</strong> the
    stakes were higher and the response worse. Italy invaded Ethiopia (Abyssinia) in October
    1935; the League declared Italy the aggressor and applied sanctions in November — but
    half-sanctions: no oil embargo, no coal, and throughout the war Britain kept the Suez
    Canal open to Italian supply ships. In December 1935 the secret Hoare-Laval plan, which
    would have rewarded Mussolini with two-thirds of Abyssinia, leaked and destroyed what
    was left of the League's credibility; Addis Ababa fell in May 1936 and sanctions were
    abandoned that July. The Italians fought with aircraft and mustard gas against an army
    with 1920s rifles; Ethiopian dead are estimated near a quarter of a million. Haile
    Selassie's warning to the Assembly — that aggressors
    everywhere had learned their lesson — was immediately proved: in March 1936, while the
    world watched Abyssinia, Hitler remilitarised the Rhineland unopposed. The League failed
    in the 1930s because its strongest members calculated that enforcing the Covenant might
    mean war with a major power, and chose national self-interest instead — while remaining
    too ashamed to admit the choice, which was the worst of both worlds: enough sanction to
    anger Mussolini, not enough to stop him. Public opinion, tellingly, was ahead of its
    governments: the 1935 British "Peace Ballot" drew over 11 million votes overwhelmingly
    in favour of collective security — the will existed below the level where decisions
    were made.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="find the faulty line — the 1930s record" widget="spot-error" config={lonFailSpot} />

  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the League failed in the 1930s because its rules were
    too weak — stronger wording would have stopped Mussolini. By 1935 every instrument
    existed: the Covenant declared Italy the aggressor and sanctions followed within
    weeks. What failed was enforcement — half-sanctions, no oil embargo, Suez left open —
    because Britain and France calculated full enforcement might mean war. These are
    lessons about the willingness of the strong, not the rules' wording.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Give two membership absences and two design features that weakened the League from the start.</li>
    <li>Why do the Aaland Islands (1921) and Greece-Bulgaria (1925) count as successes where Vilna (1920) and Corfu (1923) do not?</li>
    <li>"Weaknesses in organisation made the League's failure inevitable." How far do you agree?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Absences: the USA never joined (Senate rejection, 1919
    and 1920); the USSR excluded until 1934, Germany until 1926. Design: unanimity for
    every decision and no army — force meant members volunteering. (2) The successes
    involved small states that accepted the verdicts; Vilna and Corfu involved states
    willing to defy it (at Corfu, a great power using force) — settled around the League,
    not through it. (3) Only partly: the machinery resolved real
    disputes in 1921 and 1925, and the UN kept nearly the whole design, fixing mainly
    unanimity. It worked when the great powers used it, collapsed when enforcement would
    cost them something real — the organisation was weak; the will was weaker.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The League was a working machine that the great powers stopped operating — judge every case against it.</li>
    <li>Design set the ceiling: unanimity plus no army meant enforcement slow, collective and optional.</li>
    <li>Membership gaps (no USA, late USSR and Germany) removed the power collective security needed.</li>
    <li>The 1920s are genuinely mixed: real peacekeeping successes and humanitarian work alongside Vilna and Corfu.</li>
    <li>The Depression removed the will, the money and the atmosphere — not the rules — and made aggressors as well.</li>
    <li>Manchuria and Abyssinia proved the League would condemn and do nothing when enforcement risked war.</li>
    <li>The UN inherited the buildings, files and problem — fixing unanimity with a veto that concentrates the great-power question.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    <em>The Treaty of Versailles</em> is the prequel — the settlement the Covenant was
    written into; <em>Colonialism and Empire</em> follows the mandate system mentioned only
    in passing; and the UN's record makes a strong advanced-level comparison essay.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Two final refinements. First, membership tells its own story: the League grew from 42
    founding members to about 60 at its peak, and the dictators' exits (Japan 1933, Germany
    1933, Italy 1937) were tacit confessions that the institution's condemnation still
    stung — the USSR, admitted in 1934, was itself expelled in 1939 for invading Finland,
    the Covenant's rules applied to everyone in principle and to no one strong in practice.
    Second, judge the afterlife. The United Nations inherited the Geneva buildings and files,
    promoted the Health Organisation into the WHO, kept the ILO (which survives unchanged),
    and fixed the one procedural flaw that mattered most: instead of unanimity among all, a
    Security Council of five permanent members with vetoes — concentrating, rather than
    escaping, the problem the League died of: collective security only functions when the
    powerful agree. Historians now tend to see the League not as a foolish idea but as the
    necessary first draft — an experiment whose documented failures (unanimity, no army, no
    America) became the design requirements of everything attempted since. That is a
    defensible answer to "to what extent was the League a success": it failed as a
    peacekeeper, worked as a pioneer, and taught the world precisely what it would need to
    build next.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="drag the divider — the draft and its rewrite" widget="compare" config={lonAfterlifeCompare} />
  <p>
    If your essay needs one line to end on, make it this: the League did not die of its
    weaknesses — it died of its members' unwillingness to be strong for it. Every instrument
    it needed existed in 1935; what was missing, in London and Paris, was the decision to
    use them.
  </p>

